WiiStation CHANGE LOG:
Ver 5.7.1 (2026-10-08):
* Fixed: a game loaded after another one could stop at its first frame
  ("Lightrec: fault 6") when memory had been used in between, e.g. by two
  games from the network share in a row. The recompiler now keeps its 2.5 MB
  of state from game to game instead of asking for it again each time.
* If the recompiler ever cannot start, WiiStation now stops with "Lightrec: no
  memory for its state" instead of running the game on nothing.

Ver 5.7.0 (2026-10-08):
* Network shares work with current servers: SMB2 and SMB3 through libsmb2,
  in place of a library that spoke only SMB1 (off by default on Samba 4.11+,
  Windows 10 and 11). No password length limit; NTLMv2 logins.
* RomDirSMB: the game folders on the share, as RomDirSD (folders inside the
  share open like folders on the SD card).
* Games on the share read in small pieces after a jump and larger ones while
  they read on, and get no CD read-ahead: the Wii's Wi-Fi gives about
  350 KB/s. Expect waits in loading screens and videos; CHD images help.
* A read that fails reconnects to the share once, so a short network drop
  does not stop the game.
* "Cannot connect to the share" says the server's reason.

Ver 5.6.1 (2026-10-08):
* The settings file is wiistation/settings.ini (was settingsRX2022.cfg), and
  a game's own settings are wiistation/settings/<serial>.ini (was .cfg). The
  lines are the same; rename your file to keep it. Any INI editor can open it:
  ; comments, indented lines and [section] lines are now accepted.
* settings.ini is written in sections ([General], [Video], [Audio], ...), each
  with a short comment. example_settings.ini shows every setting, its default
  and what it does.
* RomDirSD and RomDirUSB replace RomDir1..RomDir4: a list of game folders with
  a comma between two folders, e.g. RomDirSD = "/wiistation/isos, /roms/psx".

Ver 5.6.0 (2026-10-07):
* WiiStation's folder on the SD card and USB device is now /wiistation (was
  /wiisxrx): settings, saves, save states, BIOS, fonts, languages, controller
  files and logs. Nothing is moved for you: to keep your settings, saves and
  BIOS, move or copy them from /wiisxrx to /wiistation.
* Games can be anywhere: RomDir1..RomDir4 in settingsRX2022.cfg name up to four
  folders (default sd:/wiistation/isos and usb:/wiistation/isos). Load from
  SD/USB opens the one that exists, or lists them when there are several.
  The card is FAT, which has no links or junctions; this replaces them.
* Saving settings creates /wiistation when the card does not have it yet.
* `hbc.py lastlog` shows WiiStation's output after it exits: it now leaves
  with exit(), which also lets the HBC agent write the SD card back.

Ver 5.5.0 (2026-10-07):
* The Homebrew Channel agent from HBC 1.10.0 (was 1.9.3). With HBC 1.10 on the
  Wii, `hbc.py status` now shows WiiStation's frame pacing and main stack use,
  a stack overflow, assert() or abort() is reported like a crash, and the SD
  card is written back when WiiStation exits. Reset still returns to the menu.

Ver 5.4.3 (2026-10-05):
* Configure Buttons says "PSX Port 1" where it said "PSX Pad 1", as
  Configure Input does.

Ver 5.4.2 (2026-10-05):
* Configure Buttons: each mapping button grows to fit its label ("Start+Z",
  "D-Down", "RS-Button" ran past their buttons), without touching its
  neighbours; the stacked buttons no longer overlap.
* Buttons that size themselves to their label leave a few more pixels
  around it.

Ver 5.4.1 (2026-10-04):
* Configure Buttons works with a Co-Op or Multitap port: it maps each
  player's or slot's controller in turn (Next), where it showed "No Physical
  Controller Assigned" for the port.

Ver 5.4.0 (2026-10-04):
* Co-Op: up to 8 controllers play one PlayStation port together, so more
  people can play a game made for fewer. Each player gets a layout (whole
  pad, left or right half, D-pad and sticks, action or shoulder buttons):
  Settings > Input > Configure Input, Manual, port type Co-Op, Customize.
* Configure Input laid out like the Settings tabs: "PSX Port 1/2" at the top
  of each port's column; the Multitap and Co-Op rows appear only when chosen.
* Switching Configure Input to Automatic turns a Multitap or Co-Op port back
  into a plain port (a port left as Multitap could not be seen or changed).
* Wii Remote LEDs show the player number on multitap slots and Co-Op players
  (they went dark from the third slot on).
* Settings > Input: the Controller Type buttons line up and "Stick D-pad"
  fits its button.
* A game loaded after another one starts with all of VRAM clear; the last
  few pixels of the last row kept the previous game's data.

Ver 5.3.0 (2026-10-02):
* New controller type "Stick D-pad": a digital pad whose D-pad the left stick
  also presses (8 directions), for games that read only the D-pad.
* Multitap: slots A-D start as GameCube pads 1-4, and automatic assignment
  keeps each GameCube pad in its own slot (players no longer move around when
  a pad answers late, and port 2 keeps its pad).
* Changing a port's controller type while a game runs (for example Multitap to
  GameCube pad) now works: the port is empty for half a second, as with a real
  swap, so the game finds the new controller.
* Crash Bash: the pause menu and the rules screens show their text in OpenGX
  and Soft Fast (the GPU list is walked in slices, as the console does).
* Soft Timed: the frame limiter no longer waits when the game is already late
  (Crash Bash on the Wii: 0.91x -> 1.00x).

Ver 5.2.0 (2026-10-01):
* Lightrec core synced to upstream a7464cc. On a real Wii the emulated CPU
  costs 0.3-0.8 points less load in each of 8 test games. On the PS1 test
  programs (ps1-tests) its results are the same as the old core's.
* PS-X EXE programs run on Lightrec (the CPU core was never made for them).
* Fixed two emulator crashes on unusual I/O accesses (unaligned words to
  hardware registers, 8/16-bit cache-control accesses).
* HLE BIOS printf: field flags (%-10s) and signed %d.
* Docs/ACCURACY.md: measured differences from a real PS1, and the plan for
  a Fast and an Accurate option.

Ver 5.1.0 (2026-10-01):
* VRAM fills 3-4x faster on the Wii; CPU-to-VRAM transfers copy words
  (FF7 load 65.3 -> 53.9%; mean of 8 games 38.9 -> 35.8%).
* FMV: 24-bit tiles and MDEC colour clamps faster; output unchanged.
* XA audio filter 36% cheaper; Lightrec map lookup cached.
* CD read-ahead on by default. New setting LimiterDebt (125 ms by default).
* Crash reports for hangs, guest crashes, missing audio and exceptions
  (HBC agent 1.9.3).

Ver Beta 4.5:
* Added OpenGx hardware rendering plugin.
  The original code uses GLES from pcsxRearm,
  and the OpenGx framework comes from [https://github.com/davidgfnet/opengx].
  Key fixes for GLES and OpenGx:
    Little-endian to big-endian conversion.
    Display of opaque pixels in transparent mode.
    Sub-texture pixel updates.
    Other minor fixes.
  Currently known major issues:
    Due to texture caching, sometimes cached textures are not cleared in time, resulting in incorrect rendering on the screen.
    Data transferred via the primLoadImage command occasionally fails to load correctly onto the screen.

Ver Beta 4.0:
* HID controllers support.
  (The PS2 and PS3 controllers (includes vibration) has been fully tested.)
* Using the speed limit logic of old video plugins.
* Merged some code from PCSX-ReARMed to reduce the occurrence/happening of some issues.
* Reduced the use of MEM1 memory and fixed crashes/errors in games such as Rayman 2.
* Added Gaussian interpolation to SPU as an option.
* Add the old software rendering method as the default GPU plugin. The new one
  will be kept as an option in case some games don't work correctly there.
* Update libCHDr to latest until git 07a564a.
* Update Lightrec to latest until git 8d3db1a.

Ver 3.2:
* SPU: Correction for overflow of 16 digit numbers.
* psxbios: implement yet more memcard details.
* Fixes Formula 1 freezing at first loading screen.
* Update to latest libCHDr.
* To avoid errors where CHD games occupy too much memory, move Lightrec's 4M Buffer to MEM2 memory.

Ver 3.0:
* 240p/Lightgun/Mouse/Multitap support.
* CHD v1-v5 compressed format support.
* Add Force NTSC mode.
* Rewritten PCM decoding logic using the paired single instruction,
  there is a slight increase in corresponding speed.
* Imported the latest SPU, GPU, BIOS, Cdrom and other modules from PCSX ReARMed.
* Add separate save function, each game can save its own settings.
* Add old dynamic compilation core, For games where the Lightrec core cannot run,
  you can try the old dynamic compilation core.
* Many other minor corrections.

Ver 2.4:
* Use devkitPPC r41-2 + libOGC2 to compile code and solve the problem of some games not running.
* Use the latest Lightrec to solve the problem of slow of Destruction Derby Raw.
* Fix the logic of disc swapping, and games such as Metal Gear Solid can be swapped normally.
* Reduce loading time for special games.
  Now limited to games Parasite Eve and LEGO Island 2 only, Perhaps it should be an option.
* Fix the logic of CDDA playback, no longer using multithreading.
* Fix the issue of Vib Ribbon playing Reverb sound, there are still minor issues.

Ver 2.0:
* Use a new dynamic compilation (dynarec) method Lightrec, a super cool way.
  Many games can now run at full speed.
* Add Wii Message Board support for write playlog (Thanks SuperrSonic and saulfabregwiivc).
* Import the GTE code of PCSX-ReARMed.
* Fix issues
    Batman Beyond: Return of the Joker can't be played.
    Driver 2 code dump error on mission "Chase the Gunman".
* Modify the code to support higher versions of devkitPPC.
  ※※※ Note: For issue 81, the latest high version of libOGC must be used.

Ver 1.0:
* Incorporating the CDROM and CDRISO codes of PCSX-ReARMed.
* CDDA (Compact Disc Digital Audio) tracks support.
* Incorporating the timer codes of PCSX-ReARMed.
* Combined the DFSound module of PCSX-ReARMed and used the SDL library.
* Modification of some dynamic compilation instructions.
* Support for multiple languages.
  ※※※ Note: It reads a font file in a fixed location, so make sure that [sd:/wiistation/fonts/chs.dat] exists ※※※
* For some customed Chinese culture games, specific BIOS is automatically loaded.
* Other minor corrections, such as disc changing (swap) and automatic fixes (autoFix functions) for some games.

